    <!-- basis -->
    <section class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="border-b border-ink-500 pb-3 font-display text-2xl">How to read these numbers</h2>
      <div class="mt-6 rounded border border-ink-600 bg-ink-900 p-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-bone-mute">Basis of figures</div>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed text-bone-dim">
          <li>This report covers <strong class="text-bone">2025-06-18 to 2026-09-24</strong> and is point-in-time. It is regenerated only when a full audit is run.</li>
          <li>Each position is priced from the <strong class="text-bone">open of the first session beginning after it was posted</strong>, never the same session, so no figure benefits from hindsight. Where a post named a fill price, that price is <strong class="text-bone">not</strong> used, even when it was better than the open.</li>
          <li>Where a stop was stated and the price reached it, the position is <strong class="text-bone">closed at that stop</strong> whether or not an exit was posted separately.</li>
          <li>Returns are <strong class="text-bone">dividend-adjusted</strong>. A position closed, stopped or trimmed in the channel is measured to that announced exit; a position still held is marked to 2026-09-24. Each row shows which it is.</li>
          <li>The comparison is <strong class="text-bone">SPY bought on the same date and held the identical window</strong>, computed per position rather than as one period figure.</li>
          <li>Positions are <strong class="text-bone">equal-weighted</strong>. Actual sizing varied, so this is not an account statement and does not represent any member's realised return.</li>
          <li>A position counts only where the original post states plainly that it was taken. Anything taken without saying so is not captured, making this a subset rather than a complete record.</li>
          <li>Where options were bought, the position is measured on the move in the underlying security rather than contract profit and loss, which understates the outcome in both directions.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-5 border-t border-ink-600 pt-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-bone-mute">
          <strong class="text-bone-dim">Past performance does not indicate future results.</strong> Educational
          content only, never personalised financial advice. Leaving The Matrix is not a financial adviser and
          does not make personalised recommendations. These figures describe research published to the community.
          You are responsible for your own decisions.
        </p>
